          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had reflux for years and now food sometimes sticks going down. How worrying is that, and what does guidance describe about when to investigate?",
          "answer": "You should consult a healthcare provider to evaluate your symptom of food sticking while swallowing. Although this symptom has been categorized as routine urgency, it can indicate underlying issues that require medical evaluation, especially if it is new, severe, worsening, or unexplained.\n\nGuidelines recommend investigating dysphagia, particularly if there are \"alarm features\" present, such as significant weight loss or persistent symptoms. Clinical guidelines highlight that for reflux symptoms like heartburn and regurgitation, if alarming features such as difficulty swallowing are present, further investigation like endoscopy may be necessary (Gastroenterology society clinical guideline). \n\nBoth sources agree that if your dysphagia is new or its character has changed, it is advisable to discuss it with a clinician to rule out any serious conditions. If you experience any severe discomfort or other concerning symptoms, seeking medical attention is important.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
